        "finalAnswer": "Foundry takes **six retainers per year** on purpose. Here's the reasoning behind it:\n\n**The core logic**\nPast six concurrent retainers, founder time per client drops below the threshold that makes Foundry's work distinguishable from a normal growth shop. The economics work *because* you say no to most inbound.\n\n**Six is a hard cap, not an aspiration**\nThe framing matters: it's a ceiling, not a target. Owen tracks the count, and the partners say the no. The seventh prospect gets declined — even if the fit looks right.\n\n**The three principles behind it**\n1. **Pricing** — you pick six clients a year, full stop\n2. **Channel** — you own the loop, not a channel\n3. **Boredom** — you refuse work that bores you, even at scale (Nico wrote this into the studio principles)\n\n**What it protects**\n- Depth over volume on every deliverable\n- The founder's hours stay meaningful to each client\n- Brand work that actually redirects spend (not Notion docs)\n- The studio's identity as a brain, not a vendor\n\nThe belief has been tested — the founder pushed back on it during the Helmsmark situation in Week 10, and it came up again with Saltline scope-creep. But the principle has held as studio law. Owen's now drafting v2 of the retainer SOW to make the language match the commitment.",
